{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>YHWH is a man of war.</b> Since Moshe said to them, 'YHWH will fight for you,' therefore they said: 'YHWH is a man of war.' And perhaps you will say, His warfare is with weapons of arms — therefore it says, ״ה' שמו״ — 'HaShem is His name,' that is to say, not with toil and not with exertion, but by His name; in the manner of what is written (Jeremiah 32:18): ״האל הגדול הגבור והנורא ה' צבאות שמו״, and likewise in the creation of the world it is written (Jeremiah 33:2): ״יוצר אותה להכינה ה' שמו״, that is, by His name He created them, and not with toil and not with exertion. Another interpretation of ואנוהו — 'I and Him': that is to say, I emulate Him, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 28:9): ״והלכת בדרכיו״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: 'Just as He is merciful, so you too be merciful; just as He is gracious, so you too be gracious; just as He clothes the naked, so you too clothe the naked; just as He buries the dead, so you too bury the dead.' And the meaning of this Midrash comes to allude to His being, may He be exalted, a partner with us in our exile and in our redemption — in our exile, from the language of the promise (Genesis 46:4): ״אנכי ארד עמך מצרימה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: From here — they were exiled to Egypt, the Shekhinah was with them; and in the narrative it is written (Exodus 1:1): ״את יעקב איש וביתו באו״ — 'with Jacob,' and as I hinted there; and in our redemption as well, in what is written (Exodus 12:37): ״ויסעו בני ישראל מרעמסס״, and it is written immediately (Exodus 14:19): ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״, and a full verse it is (Psalms 60:7): ״הושיעה ימינך וענני״ — the verse requires salvation even for Himself, may He be blessed. And furthermore, these are words spoken concerning the matter of exile — 'I and He': 'I' as it is written (Ezekiel 1:1): ״ואני בתוך הגולה״, and 'He' as it is written (Jeremiah 40:1): ״והוא אסור באזקים״." "<b>And from now on,</b> let it not be difficult for you, after this matter that I have explained to you, regarding what we say in prayer on the day of the great sealing: אני והו הושיעה נא — for it would seemingly have been proper to say, אני ואותך — 'I and You,' since you see that they are words uniquely designated for the matter of redemption. And furthermore, he wished to speak in the hidden mode for another reason, because He, may He be blessed, is hidden and concealed from the grasp of all who grasp; and הושיעה נא is in the direct form, to make plain that even though He is hidden and concealed, He is revealed and present in the hearts. And it is like the pattern of the blessings that they established: ברוך אתה — 'You are blessed,' in the direct form; ואשר קדשנו — 'who has sanctified us,' in the hidden form; and so too here, הושיעה נא is in the direct form, אני והו — 'I and He,' in the hidden form, that is, ממקומו — 'from His place,' for His place is not known, in the manner of what is written (Ezekiel 3:12): ״ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו״. And this is what the verse says (Psalms 145:10): ״וחסידיך יברכוכה״ — they shall bless כה — 'thus,' that is, in this manner, namely that He is both revealed and hidden. And this is what it says immediately: ״כבוד מלכותך יאמרו״, and it is written: ״להודיע לבני אדם גבורותיו וכבוד הדר מלכותו״." "<b>And know</b> that אני והו in its reckoning is אנא ה', and the word אנא is the number of the unique Name twice over, and therefore we say in prayer: אנא הושיעה נא — thus אנא ה' is the number of the unique Name three times, which comes to 78, and this is הוה ויהיה והיה, whose number is thus, and this is clear." "<b>And we have</b> yet another tradition concerning them, that they are holy Names, and they are among the 72 Names inscribed in the verses of ויסע, ויבא, ויט, and this is known to all masters of the Names, for the Name of 72 is divided into known divisions, and each and every division has a known power for a known operation, together with knowledge of their vocalization as is proper, and these two Names — each one is a head of its known division among them: this one to gather the scattered, and that one for love. And since this is so, how pleasant and how good it is that we mention them in the midst of our prayer, to seek through them mercy from HaShem, may He be blessed, that He gather our scattered ones and that He love us as in days of old. This I heard from the mouth of the sage Rabbi Yitzchak Todros, of blessed memory." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: זה אלי ואנוהו — 'This is my El and I will adorn Him' — is the Shekhinah, which is called זה — 'this' — and זאת — 'this': (Exodus 13:8) ״בעבור זה עשה ה' לי״, (Genesis 49:28) ״וזאת אשר דבר להם״, (Leviticus 26:44) ״ואף גם זאת בהיותם״ and so on — for the Shekhinah is with them in all the exiles, and upon this it is said ״ויסע מלאך האלהים״, for the salvation of the sea was through it, and it is said of it (Exodus 23:21): ״כי שמי בקרבו״. And it is אדון חולי ארץ — 'HaShem who shakes the earth' — that David mentioned, and said (Psalms 114:7): ״מלפני אדון חולי ארץ מלפני אלוה יעקב״. And from this Moshe alluded to it here: ״עזי וזמרת יה״, for indeed it is not the way of Moshe in all the Torah to designate the Name יה, but rather the full Name; and where he did mention it (Exodus 17:16): ״כי יד על כס יה״ — therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory needed to expound of it that the Name is not whole and the Throne is not whole until the seed of Amalek is blotted out. But because the Shekhinah is the attribute of judgment, therefore Moshe mentioned the Name יה, which is the attribute of judgment, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 130:3): ״אם עונות תשמר יה אדני מי יעמד״, and it is written with an alef-dalet (i.e., אדני), and in its light who can stand before the attribute of judgment? And the word תשמר is not in the direct form but in the feminine concealed form; and so it is further written (Psalms 118:18): ״יסור יסרני יה״, and it is written (Psalms 94:12): ״אשרי הגבר אשר תיסרנו יה״, and they said in Bereshit Rabbah: 'אשרי הגבר אשר תיסרנו יה — the blessed Holy One does not say הוי״ה but rather יה, like one who stands before the judge and he judges him.' End quote." "<b>And I found</b> in the Midrash Tehillim on the verse ״עזי וזמרת יה״: in this world they praise Him with two letters, but in the world to come with six letters, as it is said (Isaiah 26:4): ״כי ביה ה׳ צור עולמים״. And the intention in this Midrash, in my opinion, is that the two letters are the attribute of judgment (מדה\"ד), by which this world was created; but the world to come is through mercy, for the attribute of judgment will be elevated into mercy and the attribute of judgment will no longer operate, for at that time all will be kindness and mercy in which there is no evil inclination and no death. And this is the explanation of what they expounded in Shemot Rabbah: ״In the past I and My court walked before you״, as it is said (Exodus 13:21): ״וה׳ הולך לפניהם״; but for the time to come, ״I alone״, as it is said (Isaiah 52:12): ״כי הולך לפניכם ה׳ ומאספכם אלהי ישראל״. He is the Elohim of Jacob, and all is one attribute. ״ואנוהו״ — I will exalt Him to the highest dwelling; ״אלהי אבי״ — Moses mentioned ״the Elohim of my father״ with regard to his tribe, for the attribute of judgment (מדה\"ד) is the attribute of Levi. <b>And I will extol Him</b> with the complete Name, which is the attribute of mercy — this is what it says: <b>YHWH is a man of war, YHWH is His Name</b> — that He will be elevated through mercy. This is the unification, to bind the end with the beginning, and the conclusion with its origin. And so you find in the Book of Yetzirah: ״Understand with wisdom, be wise with understanding; establish a matter upon its essence, and restore the Former to His station״ — the meaning of ״to His station״ is above on high; and he mentioned this so as to unify the entire structure from below upward."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :register {:fixes 1}, :notes {5 "מדה\"ד appears as a standard abbreviation for מדת הדין (the attribute of judgment); rendered accordingly. The quotation from the Book of Yetzirah follows standard editions (ספר יצירה א:ד approximately)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:52.349452257Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}